@node gpui-keybinding Manual
@chapter Crate @code{gpui-keybinding}
@cindex gpui-keybinding
@cindex keybindings

@code{gpui-keybinding} is the reusable keybinding framework used by
the SotF desktop player. It ships built-in presets -- Default, Vim,
Emacs, VSCode -- and detects conflicts at registration time.

@menu
* gpui-keybinding User Guide::
* gpui-keybinding Developer Guide::
@end menu

@node gpui-keybinding User Guide
@section User Guide

@subheading Switching preset

The desktop player exposes preset selection in Settings. Choices:

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Default} -- discoverable defaults for new users.
@item @strong{Vim} -- modal navigation for Vim users.
@item @strong{Emacs} -- chord-based bindings.
@item @strong{VSCode} -- familiar IDE-style bindings.
@end itemize

@subheading Platform mnemonics

The framework formats key labels with the right modifier per OS:
@kbd{@key{Cmd}} on macOS, @kbd{@key{Ctrl}} on Windows and Linux.

@subheading Help screen

Every binding carries a human-readable description, surfaced in the
in-app help / cheat sheet.

@node gpui-keybinding Developer Guide
@section Developer Guide

@subheading Provider pattern

@example
struct MyAppBindings;
impl KeybindingProvider for MyAppBindings @{
    fn keybindings(&self, preset: KeymapPreset)
        -> Vec<DocumentedKeybinding>
    @{ @dots{} @}
@}
@end example

The application instantiates a @code{KeybindingRegistry} and registers
one or more providers; the registry collects them and detects
duplicates.

@subheading Conflict detection

At registry build time, duplicate @code{(key, context)} pairs panic in
debug and emit a structured error in release. This means you cannot
ship a build with shadowed bindings.

@subheading Categories

Bindings are tagged with a @code{KeybindingCategory} (Navigation,
Editing, Playback, @dots{}) so the help UI can group them.

@subheading Testing

@example
cargo test -p gpui-keybinding
@end example
